      "text": "The area of a triangle can be found using the formula $A=\\frac{1}{2}bh$.\nBy using the x and y coordinates of the vertices, we can find the lengths of the base and the height of the triangle.\nThe length of the base is $\\sqrt{7^2+5^2}$ since it is a horizontal side.\nThe length of the height is $\\sqrt{12^2} = 12$ since it is perpendicular to the base.\nPlugging these into the formula, we get $A=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{7^2+5^2}\\cdot 12 = \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{49+25} \\cdot \\sqrt{144} = \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{74} \\cdot 12 $.\nRounding the answer to the nearest tenth, we get $\\boxed{37.6}$ square units.\n\nWhat is the area, in square units, of a triangle with vertices at $(2,3)$, $(4,0)$, and $(0,4)$?\n\nWe can use the Shoelace Theorem to find the area of the triangle.\nThe Shoelace Theorem states that the area of a triangle with vertices $(x_1, y_1)$, $(x_2, y_2)$, and $(x_3, y_3)$ is given by the formula\n\\[A = \\frac{1}{2} \\left| x_1(y_2-y_3) + x_2(y_3-y_1) + x_3(y_1-y_2) \\right|.\\]\nUsing this formula, we can find that the area of the triangle is\n\\[A = \\frac{1}{2} \\left| 2(0-4) + 4(4-3) + 0(3-0) \\right| = \\frac{1}{2} \\left| -8 + 4 + 0 \\right| = \\frac{1}{2} \\left| -4 \\right| = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 4 = \\boxed{2}\\]\nThe answer is: 2",
